Section 3: Generalized Preparation Protocols Across Product Categories
To ensure operational efficiency across commercial bakery lines, processing procedures are standardized into five primary product categories based on structural chemistry and thermal kill-step methods [1].


Category
	Representative Products
	Primary Processing Sequence
	Thermal & Physical Target Parameters
	Category A: Yeast-Leavened Breads
	Pandesal, Buns, Star Bread, Spanish Bread, Putok, Ensaymada
	1. Spiral Mixing & Hydration -> 2. Roller Refining -> 3. Division & Shaping -> 4. Proofing -> 5. Oven Baking
	Exit temp 26°C to 28°C; proof at 30°C and 80% RH; core bake temp 95°C or higher
	Category B: Deep-Fried Delicacies
	Binangkal, Shakoy Bread, Yeast Doughnuts
	1. Mixing & Hydration -> 2. Portioning & Shaping -> 3. Proofing / Coating -> 4. Deep Frying
	Fry at 165°C to 175°C; core temp 95°C or higher
	Category C: Laminated Pastries
	Otap, Hopia / Pig-Pie, Hopia Panet
	1. Two-Phase Dough/Fat Mixing -> 2. Multi-Fold Envelope Lamination -> 3. Log Rolling & Slicing -> 4. Baking
	Sheet fat paste evenly; steam-lift expansion at 175°C to 190°C
	Category D: Creamed Pastries & Cookies
	Cheese Bar, Half Moon, Kringcles
	1. Fat & Sugar Creaming -> 2. Liquid Emulsification -> 3. Dry Integration -> 4. Chilling / Sheet Panning -> 5. Baking
	Cream 5 to 8 mins; chill crinkle dough at 2°C to 4°C; bake at 160°C to 175°C
	Category E: Custards & Sweets
	Leche Flan, Pudding, Bagong Bayan, Double Body
	1. Mass Blending / Straining -> 2. Molding / Sheet Pressing -> 3. Steam Baking or Double Toasting
	Strain liquids twice; steam/bake at 160°C to 175°C; double-toast at 135°C
	3.1 Category A: Yeast-Leavened Commercial Breads
1. Compounding & Spiral Hydration: Load sifted flours, milk solids, dough improver, and sugars into the spiral mixer bowl [1]. Add calculated water (or ice blend), liquid eggs, and rehydrated yeast [1, 5]. Mix at low speed (30 to 60 RPM) for 3 to 5 minutes until dry particles are fully hydrated [1, 5].
2. Gluten Matrix Kneading: Add salt and fats (shortening or margarine) [1]. Increase speed to medium (120 to 180 RPM) and knead for 8 to 12 minutes until gluten reaches full development (passing the windowpane test, where dough stretches thin without tearing) [1, 15].
3. Mechanical Dough Refining: Pass the dough mass through an industrial roller-kneader/refiner 15 to 25 times [1, 4]. This mechanical shearing compresses air cells, homogenizes fat distribution, and creates a dense, fine crumb structure [1, 4].
4. Division, Shaping & Panning: Portion dough on bench scales [1]. Shape into target profiles (trapezoidal rolls for Pandesal, coiled spirals for Ensaymada, scissor-cut ovals for Star Bread/Putok) [1, 4]. Arrange on sanitized aluminum baking sheets with adequate expansion spacing [1, 2].
5. Controlled Proofing: Place panned trays into the Proofing Chamber maintained at 28°C to 32°C and 75% to 85% relative humidity [1]. Proof until volume expands 80% to 100% (45 to 90 minutes) [1, 4].
6. Thermal Kill-Step Baking (CCP-1): Bake in rotary or rack ovens at 175°C to 190°C (350°F to 375°F) [1, 2]. Internal core temperature must reach 95°C or higher measured via calibrated digital probe thermometer to ensure starch gelatinization, gluten setting, and destruction of vegetative pathogens [1, 6].
3.2 Category B: Deep-Fried Direct-Heat Delicacies
1. Mixing & Hydration: Combine sifted dry ingredients with liquid bases (water, milk, eggs, oil) until a firm, plastic dough forms [1, 4]. Avoid over-kneading chemically leavened fried doughs to prevent toughness [1, 8].
2. Portioning & Shaping: Divide dough into spherical balls (Binangkal), twisted ropes (Shakoy), or stamped rings (Doughnuts) [1, 4].
3. Surface Application & Proofing: For Binangkal, pass dough balls briefly through a water bath and roll in raw sesame seeds [1, 4]. For yeasted Shakoy and Doughnuts, proof at 30°C for 30 to 45 minutes until light and airy [1, 4].
4. Thermal Frying Execution: Submerge dough units in deep-fat frying vats with clean vegetable oil pre-heated to 165°C to 175°C (330°F to 350°F) [1, 4]. Fry for 1.5 to 4 minutes per side, flipping continuously until golden brown [1, 4]. Internal core temperature must exceed 95°C [1].
5. Draining & Finishing: Drain excess surface oil on wire racks for 3 minutes [1]. Roll warm goods in white sugar, dusting powder, or glazes [1, 4].
3.3 Category C: Laminated Pastries & Flaky Goods
1. Dough Phase Separation: Prepare the primary base dough (détrempe) using 1st Class flour, water, yeast, sugar, and salt; prepare the secondary fat paste (beurrage) using 3rd Class flour, shortening, and butter/margarine [1, 4].
2. Multi-Fold Lamination: Sheet primary dough to 6 mm thickness [1]. Spread secondary fat paste evenly across two-thirds of the dough sheet [1]. Execute a series of 3-fold envelope turns, sheeting between folds to create alternating micro-layers of dough and fat [1, 4].
3. Log Rolling, Slicing & Coating: Tightly roll the laminated sheet into a dense cylinder [1, 4]. Slice transversely into disk portions, roll out thin, enclose filling (if applicable), or coat both surfaces in coarse granulated sugar [1, 4].
4. Baking & Toasting: Bake at 175°C to 190°C [1, 4]. Water locked within fat layers vaporizes into steam, pushing dough layers apart before evaporating, producing a light, flaky structure [1, 4].
3.4 Category D: Batters, Creamed Cookies & Sheet Pastries
1. Emulsified Creaming: Cream softened butter or margarine with sugars in a planetary mixer fitted with a paddle attachment at medium speed for 5 to 8 minutes until light and aerated [1, 4]. Add eggs gradually, ensuring full emulsification [1, 4].
2. Dry Integration: Sift dry ingredients (3rd Class flour, cocoa powder, milk powder, baking powder, salt) [1, 4]. Fold into creamed mixture at low speed just until integrated [1]. Over-mixing develops unwanted gluten, causing cookies and bars to become tough and rubbery [1, 7].
3. Chilling, Portioning & Baking: For crinkle cookies, chill dough at 2°C to 4°C for 2 hours to firm fats before shaping and sugar coating [1, 4]. For sheet bars, spread batter into parchment-lined pans [1, 4]. Bake at 160°C to 175°C until center springs back [1].
3.5 Category E: Custards, Sweets & Reconstituted Goods
1. Custard Blending & Straining: Dissolve sugars in liquid milks and combine gently with whisked eggs [1, 4]. Avoid incorporating air bubbles, which cause unsightly voids in baked custards [1]. Pass liquid through fine-mesh cheesecloth twice [1, 4].
2. Reconstituted Sweet Blending: Blend fine toasted breadcrumbs with water, sugar, flavorings, and fats into a smooth, dense paste [1, 4].
3. Thermal Processing: Steam or bake custards in a water bath (bain-marie) at 160°C until protein matrix coagulates [1, 4]. Bake or double-toast reconstituted pastes in shallow pans until firm and golden [1, 4].


